=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The RIP daemon on router0 is running but has no network statements in its
configuration. The FRR running-config shows only `router rip` / `exit` — no
`network` clauses whatsoever — whereas every peer router (router1, router5,
router6, etc.) carries explicit `network` blocks that tie their interfaces
into the RIP domain. Because router0 has no network bindings, FRR never
enables RIP on any of its interfaces: it neither sends nor listens for RIP
advertisements on eth0.20 (172.16.20.1, the VLAN 20 office gateway), eth0.10
(172.16.10.1, VLAN 10), eth2 (10.16.1.1, the WAN link to router1), or eth4
(172.20.1.1, the link to router5).

The consequence is two-fold. First, router0 has no RIP-learned routes and no
default route, so all traffic from laptop0's office (172.16.20.0/24,
172.16.10.0/24) that is not covered by the two hand-crafted static kernel
routes is silently dropped at router0. Second — and equally important — none
of the downstream routers (router1, router2, router3, router6) have ever
received an advertisement for 172.16.x.x, so even the few static-routed
forward paths that do escape router0 fail on the return trip: reply packets
are dropped at router6 or router3 because those routers have no route back to
the office subnets.

Root cause: router0's `router rip` stanza is empty — the network statements
that would activate RIP on its interfaces were never added.

Corrective fix: log into router0 via vtysh and add the missing network
statements, e.g.:
  router rip
   network 10.0.0.0/8
   network 172.16.0.0/16
   network 172.20.0.0/16
   network 192.168.0.0/16
   version 2
This will let router0 exchange RIP routes with router1 (via 10.16.1.0/24) and
router5 (via 172.20.1.0/24), advertise the two office VLANs to the full
routing domain, and receive the default route or remote prefixes it currently
lacks.
=== END ===
```